{-
   ==========================================================================================
   What a run looks like to the person who started it - Design-Test.md §5.5.
   ==========================================================================================

   The report stream is what a *parent* reads and this is what a *human* reads, and the two are
   deliberately not the same text. §5.2's stream is line-oriented, unbuffered and written as it
   happens, because its whole property is that a case which takes the process down still names
   itself; a human wants the opposite of every one of those things - the cases in a table, the
   failures gathered at the end, and colour to tell them apart at a glance.

   So this module holds the second one, and the first is untouched. Nothing here is on the path of a
   run whose report has a channel of its own: a driver's fixture builds a `Console` that reports
   nothing and every function below returns at its first line - see `openConsole`.

   **Three things a terminal buys that a log cannot, and each is one field of `Terminal`:**

    - *while it runs*, one line that rewrites itself - which case is running, how far in, how long.
      A crash therefore leaves the name of the case that died on the screen, which is the same
      property §5.2 buys with a `begin` that has no `end`, obtained here for a human with no second
      process to read anything;
    - *when it finishes*, the cases as a table. A column of times is a column somebody scans for the
      outlier, which a run of `pass Show.boolText` lines is not;
    - *colour*, which is what makes "1 failed" findable in a screen of green.

   **A table stops being a table somewhere above a screenful, and the run rolls up by module.** §5.5's
   worked example is a suite of five cases and the table is exactly right for it; the standard
   library's own suite is two hundred and thirty-four, run eight times over by `test/run-base.sh`, and
   listing every one of them printed two thousand lines of the word *passed* - which is the same
   mistake the design refuses in the other direction, a report nobody reads. So above `perCaseLimit`
   the rows are one per *module* with its counts and its time, which is fifteen lines that answer the
   question the table was for; the failures underneath are unaffected, because they are the part a
   reader came for and there are never two hundred of them. `-v` asks for every case whatever the
   count.

   **And on everything that is not a terminal, none of it happens.** Not "a degraded version": the
   progress line is not written at all, the table is drawn in ASCII with no escape sequences in it,
   and what a pipe receives is the same text a terminal would show minus the colour. That is one
   `Terminal` value doing the deciding rather than a flag tested at each of the twenty places that
   print - Terminal.yana's opening note is the argument for it.
-}

import Native
import Terminal

{-
   The reporter, and the states it can be in.

   `reporting` is the whole switch: a run whose report is being read by something else builds a
   console with it false, and every function here begins by testing it. `terse` is `-q` - the
   failures as they happen and nothing else, which is what a shard is run with because its parent is
   the thing that reports. `progress` is the in-place line, which needs a terminal *and* a run that
   is not printing a case per line underneath it. `perCase` is which of the two tables the run ends
   with - see `perCaseLimit`.

   `painted` is when the progress line was last written, and it is here rather than in `Status`
   because the clock is: Terminal.yana asks the operating system nothing, which is the property that
   makes every function in it testable, and this module already reads `monotonicNanos` for the run's
   own duration.
-}
pub data Console {
    terminal: Terminal,
    status: Status,
    results: Table,
    tallies: [ModuleTally],
    failures: String,
    reporting: Bool,
    terse: Bool,
    progress: Bool,
    verbose: Bool,
    perCase: Bool,
    ran: Size,
    passed: Size,
    failed: Size,
    skipped: Size,
    total: Size,
    started: I64,
    painted: I64
}

{-
   One module's cases, added up - the row of the table a run too long to list draws instead.

   The counts and not the cases: what a reader wants from two hundred rows of *passed* is which
   module they were in and how long it took, and both of those are three numbers rather than a list.
   A module that lost nothing is one line; a module that lost something is one line and the failures
   in full underneath, which is where the detail was going to be read anyway.

   The name is set once, at the push, and only the numbers move after that - which is why they are
   scalars rather than anything that would have to be re-owned in place.
-}
pub data ModuleTally {name: String, passed: Size, failed: Size, skipped: Size, nanos: I64}

{-
   How many cases a run may have before its table becomes one row per module.

   A fixed number rather than the terminal's height, deliberately: the same run in two windows should
   print the same report, and a threshold that moved with the window would make the two
   incomparable. Thirty is about a screen and is comfortably above every suite the design's §5.5
   example is written for.
-}
fn perCaseLimit() -> Size = 30

{-
   How long the progress line is allowed to stand before it is redrawn - one frame, at sixty hertz.

   Without it the line is rewritten once per *case*, and the standard library's suite runs two
   hundred and thirty-four of them in eighty milliseconds: three writes a millisecond, which no
   terminal can draw and which reads as a strobe rather than as progress. What the throttle costs is
   stated rather than hidden - the name on the line may be up to one frame behind the case that is
   running, so a case that dies within sixteen milliseconds of the last redraw is attributed to a
   case slightly before it. Every case slower than a frame is named before it runs, which is the half
   of §5.5's crash guarantee that was ever load-bearing: a case that hangs is a case that is slow.
-}
fn frameNanos() -> I64 = 16000000

{-
   The terminal a run should report to, which is the detected one with the command line applied.

   The detection answers what the *stream* is and the switches answer what the user wants, in that
   order, and only the switches can turn something on. `--color always` is the pager case and is the
   reason the field is a choice rather than a `Bool`: a program that must be told twice - once to
   turn detection off and once to turn colour on - has a switch nobody can remember.
-}
pub fn consoleTerminal(options: Options) -> Terminal:
    let &terminal = detectTerminal()

    if options.color == ColorNever then terminal = terminal.withoutColor()
    if options.color == ColorAlways && terminal.depth == NoColor then terminal.depth = BrightColor
    if options.ascii then terminal = terminal.withoutUnicode()
    if options.noProgress then terminal = terminal.quietly()

    return terminal

{-
   The console for a run, and the header it prints.

   `reporting` is the runner's `silent` inverted and is handed in rather than worked out here: what
   makes a process silent is descriptor 3 and `--only`, which are §5.3's business and not this
   module's.

   The header names the module when every case in the selection shares one, which is the ordinary
   case - a test binary is usually one suite - and says only the count when they do not. Naming what
   is about to run is what makes a run that selected nothing obvious *before* it says so.
-}
pub fn openConsole(options: Options, ->title: String, total: Size, reporting: Bool) -> Console:
    let terminal = consoleTerminal(options)

    if reporting && !options.quiet then announce(terminal, title, total)

    {-
       The stream and the console are two reports of the same events, and the console takes the
       channel wherever the two would land in the same place.

       Two ways they can. Natively it is a terminal: the stream has no descriptor of its own, so it
       goes to standard error, which is also where the person watching is looking. On JavaScript
       there is only `console.log` and it is *every* run - which is what printed a `begin`/`end` pair
       around every row of the table in the report `test/run-base.sh` ends with, three hundred lines
       of a stream written for a reader that was not there.

       Refused where anything else is reading it; see `muteReport`.
    -}
    let shared = terminal.interactive || reportSharesOutput()
    if reporting && shared && !options.quiet then muteReport()

    let &console = Console {
        terminal: terminal,
        status: statusOn(terminal),
        results: newTable(terminal, RuleFrame),
        tallies: [],
        failures: "",
        reporting: reporting,
        terse: options.quiet,
        progress: terminal.interactive && !options.verbose && !options.quiet,
        verbose: options.verbose,
        -- Decided once, from the count the runner already knows, rather than at the end from what
        -- arrived: a table whose shape is settled before the first case is one the rows can be
        -- built straight into.
        perCase: options.verbose || total <= perCaseLimit(),
        ran: 0, passed: 0, failed: 0, skipped: 0, total: total,
        started: monotonicNanos(),
        painted: 0
    }

    console.results.addColumn("", AlignCenter)
    console.results.addColumn("test")
    console.results.addColumn("time", AlignRight)
    console.results.addColumn("result")

    return console

{-
   What is about to run, named before it runs.

   Naming it is what makes a run that selected nothing obvious *before* the count says so, and it is
   the one line a reader has to compare against what they asked for. A title of nothing - a selection
   crossing several modules - prints the count alone rather than an arbitrary one of them.
-}
fn announce(terminal: Terminal, title: String, total: Size) -> {}:
    let &word = "tests" :: String
    if total == (1 :: Size) then word = "test"

    let count = terminal.bolded("{total}")

    if length(title) > (0 :: Size):
        let named = terminal.paint(boldColored(Cyan), title)
        print("\n{named}  {count} {word}\n\n")
    else:
        print("\nrunning {count} {word}\n\n")

{-
   The case that is about to run, on the line that rewrites itself.

   The wheel turns once per *redraw*, which is once per frame - see `frameNanos`, which is also where
   the one thing the throttle costs is written down. It is still a progress display rather than an
   animation: nothing moves it but a case beginning, so a case that hangs leaves the wheel where it
   stopped with its own name beside it, which is the crash and hang report.

   The line is not built at all for a frame that is not going to be drawn. That is the point of
   testing the clock here rather than inside `Status.update`: the formatting, the bar and the clip
   are most of the cost of a progress display, and skipping the write while still doing them would
   have saved the terminal and not the run.

   The bar is dropped when the line would not fit rather than truncated, because a truncated bar
   reads as a full one. The name is what matters and is what survives.
-}
pub fn Console.caseBegan(&self: Console, name: String) -> {}:
    if !self.reporting || !self.progress then return

    -- The first case is always drawn, so a run that is over inside one frame still showed something.
    let now = monotonicNanos()
    if self.ran > (0 :: Size) && now - self.painted < frameNanos() then return
    self.painted = now

    let wheel = self.status.spinner()
    let counted = "{self.ran + (1 :: Size)}/{self.total}"

    -- Half the width for the bar, at most twenty columns, and none at all on a narrow terminal:
    -- what the line is for is the name, and everything else gives way to it.
    let &line = "{wheel}  {counted}  " :: String
    let used = displayWidth(line) + displayWidth(name)

    if self.terminal.columns > used + (24 :: Size):
        let bar = self.terminal.bar(self.ran, self.total, 20)
        let shown = self.terminal.dimmed(bar)
        line = "{wheel}  {counted}  {shown}  "

    line ++= "{name}"
    self.status.update(line)

{-
   A case that held.

   `note` is whatever the runner has to add and is empty for almost every case: the one that carries
   anything is an `@test(aborts)` that stopped in a child, where *"(stopped, as expected)"* is the
   difference between a pass and the same case having quietly not run.

-}
pub fn Console.casePassed(&self: Console, name: String, nanos: I64, note: String) -> {}:
    self.ran = self.ran + 1
    self.passed = self.passed + 1
    if !self.reporting || self.terse then return

    self.tally(name, nanos, PassedCase)

    let g = self.terminal.glyphs()
    if self.perCase then self.addResult(copy(g.pass), name, nanos, "passed{note}", colored(Green))

    if self.verbose:
        let mark = self.terminal.inColor(Green, g.pass)
        let took = self.terminal.dimmed(durationText(nanos, self.terminal.unicode))
        print("  {mark}  {name}  {took}\n")

{-
   A case that did not hold, which is the one thing this module prints twice.

   Once in the table, so that the run's shape is one glance, and once underneath in full, because a
   table cell is not where a location and two values go. The detail comes from the recorder rather
   than from the runner - Report.yana's `currentDetail` - so a failure says the same thing here that
   it says on the stream, with the ordinal taken off and the colour put on.
-}
pub fn Console.caseFailed(&self: Console, name: String, nanos: I64, why: String) -> {}:
    self.ran = self.ran + 1
    self.failed = self.failed + 1
    if !self.reporting then return

    if self.terse:
        -- What a shard prints: the name, as it happens, and nothing that would have to be gathered.
        -- Its parent is what reports.
        print("FAIL {name}: {why}\n")
        return

    self.tally(name, nanos, FailedCase)

    let g = self.terminal.glyphs()
    if self.perCase then self.addResult(copy(g.fail), name, nanos, "failed", boldColored(Red))

    let mark = self.terminal.paint(boldColored(Red), g.fail)
    let named = self.terminal.bolded(name)
    self.failures ++= "  {mark}  {named}\n"

    let detail = currentDetail()
    if length(detail) > (0 :: Size):
        self.failures ++= indented(detail, "      ")
    else:
        let quiet = self.terminal.dimmed(why)
        self.failures ++= "      {quiet}\n"

    self.failures ++= "\n"

{-
   A case this target could not ask - an `@test(aborts)` where there is no harness to run it in.

   Counted apart from a pass and from a failure, and shown, which is the whole point: a suite that
   quietly ran fewer cases here than it ran somewhere else is the empty-corpus mistake in miniature.
-}
pub fn Console.caseSkipped(&self: Console, name: String, why: String) -> {}:
    self.ran = self.ran + 1
    self.skipped = self.skipped + 1
    if !self.reporting || self.terse then return

    self.tally(name, 0, SkippedCase)

    let g = self.terminal.glyphs()
    if self.perCase then self.addResult(copy(g.skip), name, 0, "skipped", colored(Yellow))

    if self.verbose:
        let mark = self.terminal.inColor(Yellow, g.skip)
        let quiet = self.terminal.dimmed(why)
        print("  {mark}  {name}  {quiet}\n")

fn Console.addResult(&self: Console, ->mark: String, name: String, nanos: I64, ->verdict: String,
                     style: Style) -> {}:
    self.results.addStyled(mark, style)
    self.results.add(copy(name))

    self.results.addStyled(durationText(nanos, self.terminal.unicode), dimStyle())

    self.results.addStyled(verdict, style)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The roll-up, for a run too long to list
   ==========================================================================================
-}

-- What a case came to, as the three things a tally counts. A choice rather than three functions,
-- because every one of them adds the same time to the same row and differs in one field.
pub data Outcome = PassedCase | FailedCase | SkippedCase

{-
   One case, added to its module's row.

   The row is found by name rather than by an index the runner would have to carry, and the scan is
   almost never a scan: the cases arrive in corpus order, so the module a case belongs to is the one
   the last case belonged to for every case but the first of each module. That is what the test of
   the last row is - a linear walk of fifteen entries per case would also be free, and this says
   what the shape of the input actually is.
-}
fn Console.tally(&self: Console, name: String, nanos: I64, outcome: Outcome) -> {}:
    -- `owner` rather than `module`, which is a reserved word - the same rename `Case.moduleName`
    -- carries, and for the same reason.
    let owner = moduleOf(name)
    let count = length(self.tallies)
    let &at = count

    if count > (0 :: Size) && self.tallies[count - 1].name == owner:
        at = count - 1
    else:
        for i in 0 .. count:
            if self.tallies[i].name == owner then at = i

    if at == count:
        push(self.tallies, ModuleTally {name: copy(owner), passed: 0, failed: 0, skipped: 0, nanos: 0})

    self.tallies[at].nanos = self.tallies[at].nanos + nanos

    match outcome:
        PassedCase -> self.tallies[at].passed = self.tallies[at].passed + 1
        FailedCase -> self.tallies[at].failed = self.tallies[at].failed + 1
        SkippedCase -> self.tallies[at].skipped = self.tallies[at].skipped + 1

{-
   The module half of a qualified name, which is everything before the last dot.

   The last one and not the first: a module name may have dots in it - `Native.Thread` - and a case
   name may not, because it is a declaration name. So the final dot is the join `qualifiedName` made
   and there is no ambiguity to resolve. A name with no dot at all is its own module, which is what a
   caller outside the runner would hand over.
-}
fn moduleOf(name: String) -> String:
    match findLastAscii(name, 46):
        Just(at) -> return String.slice(name, 0, at)
        Nothing -> return copy(name)

{-
   The tallies as the table a long run ends with.

   The same four columns as the per-case table and the same frame, so that the two reports read as
   one report at two scales rather than as two different tools: a mark, what it is about, how long
   it took, and what happened.
-}
fn Console.moduleTable(self: Console) -> String:
    if length(self.tallies) == (0 :: Size) then return ""

    let g = self.terminal.glyphs()
    let &table = newTable(self.terminal, RuleFrame)
    table.indent = 2

    table.addColumn("", AlignCenter)
    table.addColumn("module")
    table.addColumn("time", AlignRight)
    table.addColumn("result")

    for row in items(self.tallies):
        if row.failed > (0 :: Size):
            table.addStyled(copy(g.fail), boldColored(Red))
        else if row.passed == (0 :: Size) && row.skipped > (0 :: Size):
            table.addStyled(copy(g.skip), colored(Yellow))
        else:
            table.addStyled(copy(g.pass), colored(Green))

        table.add(copy(row.name))
        table.addStyled(durationText(row.nanos, self.terminal.unicode), dimStyle())
        table.addStyled(tallyText(row), if row.failed > (0 :: Size) then boldColored(Red) else colored(Green))

    return "{table.render()}\n"

-- One row's counts, with the parts that are zero left out - the same rule the summary line below
-- follows, and for the same reason: "12 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped" is three facts where one is
-- being said.
fn tallyText(row: ModuleTally) -> String:
    let &text = "" :: String

    if row.failed > (0 :: Size) then text ++= "{row.failed} failed"

    if row.passed > (0 :: Size):
        if length(text) > (0 :: Size) then text ++= ", "
        text ++= "{row.passed} passed"

    if row.skipped > (0 :: Size):
        if length(text) > (0 :: Size) then text ++= ", "
        text ++= "{row.skipped} skipped"

    return text

{-
   The end of the run: the table, the failures in full, and one line that says what happened.

   The order is deliberate and is the opposite of the order the events arrived in. A reader looks at
   the last line first, so the summary is last; a reader who then wants to know *which* looks up, and
   the failures are directly above it rather than scrolled off the top among the passes. That is why
   the failures are gathered here instead of printed as they happen - the one thing the stream must
   not do, and the one thing a screen must.
-}
pub fn Console.close(&self: Console, stale: Int, note: String) -> {}:
    self.status.clear()
    if !self.reporting || self.terse then return

    let elapsed = monotonicNanos() - self.started

    {-
       **The whole report is one write.**

       Natively that is one `write` where it used to be one per row, which is worth having and is not
       the reason. The reason is the other target: `print` there is `console.log`, which supplies a
       line ending of its own, so a report printed a line at a time arrives double-spaced - two
       hundred and thirty-four rows became four hundred and sixty-eight lines, and every blank line
       in the layout became two. One string printed once has one line ending added to the end of it,
       which is the one this function was going to write anyway.
    -}
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(2048)

    if self.perCase:
        if self.results.rowCount() > (0 :: Size):
            self.results.indent = 2
            out.push("{self.results.render()}\n")
    else:
        out.push(self.moduleTable())

    -- Moved rather than copied: this is the last read of the field, and `close` ends the console.
    if length(self.failures) > (0 :: Size) then out.push(exchange(self.failures, ""))

    -- The counts, in the order they matter: what went wrong, then what did not, then what was not
    -- asked. A part that is zero is left out rather than printed as a zero, so the ordinary line is
    -- "12 passed" and the width of the line says something on its own.
    let &parts = "" :: String
    if self.failed > (0 :: Size) then parts ++= self.terminal.paint(boldColored(Red), "{self.failed} failed")

    if self.passed > (0 :: Size):
        if length(parts) > (0 :: Size) then parts ++= ", "
        parts ++= self.terminal.inColor(Green, "{self.passed} passed")

    if self.skipped > (0 :: Size):
        if length(parts) > (0 :: Size) then parts ++= ", "
        parts ++= self.terminal.inColor(Yellow, "{self.skipped} skipped")

    if length(parts) == (0 :: Size) then parts ++= self.terminal.dimmed("nothing ran")

    let took = self.terminal.dimmed("in {durationText(elapsed, self.terminal.unicode)}")
    let g = self.terminal.glyphs()
    let mark = self.verdictMark(g)

    out.push("  {mark}  {parts} {took}{note}\n")

    if stale > 0:
        let word = if stale == 1 then "snapshot is" else "snapshots are"
        let said = self.terminal.inColor(Yellow, "{stale} {word} stale")
        out.push("  {said} - no test compared against them. `--update --prune` deletes them\n")

    out.push("\n")
    print("{out}")

fn Console.verdictMark(self: Console, g: Glyphs) -> String:
    if self.failed > (0 :: Size) then return self.terminal.paint(boldColored(Red), g.fail)
    if self.passed == (0 :: Size) then return self.terminal.inColor(Yellow, g.skip)
    return self.terminal.paint(boldColored(Green), g.pass)

{-
   The module every selected case belongs to, or nothing when they do not agree.

   What the header names. A test binary is usually one suite, so this is usually the suite's name and
   is worth saying; a binary that holds several, or a `--filter` that crossed them, gets a count and
   no name rather than an arbitrary one of them.
-}
pub fn sharedModule(cases: [Case], selected: [Size]) -> String:
    if length(selected) == (0 :: Size) then return ""

    let name = cases[selected[0]].moduleName
    for i in 1 .. length(selected):
        if cases[selected[i]].moduleName != name then return ""

    return copy(name)

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The two things a report has to format
   ==========================================================================================
-}

{-
   A duration, in the unit that puts two or three significant digits in front of a reader.

   Integer arithmetic rather than a division into a `Double`, and not for speed: `0.1 ms` printed
   from a float is `0.09999999999999999` on one of the two targets and `0.1` on the other, and a
   test framework whose *timings* differ between backends is one whose golden files do. Two decimals
   from a remainder are the same two decimals everywhere.

   The microsecond sign is `us` where the terminal cannot draw a `µ`, which is the same fallback the
   glyphs are and is here rather than in `Terminal` because it is a unit rather than a mark.
-}
pub fn durationText(nanos: I64, unicode: Bool) -> String:
    let value = nanos
    -- Nothing rather than "0 ns" for a case that was never run, which is what a skipped row wants:
    -- an empty cell reads as "this did not happen" where a zero reads as "this was instant".
    if value <= 0 then return ""
    if value < 1000 then return "{value} ns"

    let &micro = "us" :: String
    if unicode then micro = "µs"

    if value < 1000000 then return "{fraction(value, 1000)} {micro}"
    if value < 1000000000 then return "{fraction(value, 1000000)} ms"
    return "{fraction(value, 1000000000)} s"

-- `value / unit` to two decimal places, without ever building a float. The remainder is scaled by a
-- hundred before it is divided, so the digits are the ones a reader would get by hand.
fn fraction(value: Long, unit: Long) -> String:
    let whole = value / unit
    let hundredths = ((value % unit) * 100) / unit

    if hundredths < 10 then return "{whole}.0{hundredths}"
    return "{whole}.{hundredths}"

-- Every line of `text` with `margin` in front of it, which is what puts a recorder's failure lines
-- under the name of the case they belong to. A trailing newline does not produce an indented empty
-- line, because a block that ends in one is a block rather than a line that is missing.
pub fn indented(text: String, margin: String) -> String:
    let &out = String.ofCapacity(length(text) + length(margin))

    -- Neither operand is copied, and `push` is why: `String.push(&self: String, other: String)`
    -- takes its right operand *by loan* and block-copies the units out of it, so a `copy` in front
    -- of one is a second allocation and a second block copy of what the first copy already made.
    -- `line` is what `lines` hands over - an `iter fn` whose yield type is `->String` gives the part
    -- away - and it is pushed on the same terms.
    for line in lines(text):
        if length(line) > (0 :: Size):
            out.push(margin)
            out.push(line)
            out.push("\n")

    return out
